# localclock.cu — GPU batch execution, Tesla V100-SXM2-16GB (80 SMs, cc 7.0)
# vast.ai, 2026-08-02. degree=7 T=1.0 lookahead=0.050
# CPU column is single-threaded reference on the same host.

       N    events    rounds  mean_batch     cpu_s     gpu_s  speedup
    1536      4989      5463        58.5      0.50      0.31      1.6x
    6144     19953     14745        86.7      5.57      0.83      6.7x
   24576     79810     51710        98.6         -      2.95         -
   98304    319210    199198       102.4         -     22.52         -

# CORRECTNESS (separate run, same binary):
#   max|gpu - cpu| = 8.882e-16 at N=1536 and N=6144
#   = 4 x DBL_EPSILON. CUDA's double sin() is not required to be
#   bit-identical to glibc's; this is a transcendental-library
#   difference, NOT a scheduling difference. The conflict-free claim is
#   about the SCHEDULE and it holds: same events, same order, same
#   batches. A boolean EXACT/DIFFERS could not distinguish this from a
#   real bug, which is why the first run was uninformative.

# THE LIMIT — batch width SATURATES, it does not scale with N:
#   N=1536 -> 58.5,  6144 -> 86.7,  24576 -> 98.6,  98304 -> 102.4
# The CPU measurement (N=48..1536, 5.18 -> 83.5) was the RISING part of
# this curve and extrapolating it to ~1e4 at N=1e6 was wrong. The plateau
# is ~100 independent events per round regardless of system size.
#
# A V100 has 80 SMs and wants O(1e4-1e5) resident threads. ~100 is three
# orders short, so the device is almost entirely idle: at N=98304 the run
# is 199198 sequential rounds, each with THREE kernel launches and TWO
# blocking device->host copies (the `any` and `cnt` readbacks). At ~30 us
# of launch+sync latency per round that alone is ~20 s, which is the
# entire measured 22.52 s. The scheme as written is LATENCY-BOUND, not
# throughput-bound, and no amount of GPU helps that.
#
# Why the width saturates is the open question. It is not the maximal
# independent set that limits it — an MIS on this conflict graph would be
# ~NT/20, i.e. ~16000 at N=98304. It is the LOCAL-TIME eligibility
# window: with lookahead 0.05 and steps ~0.02, only events inside a
# narrow time band are ever eligible at once, and that band holds a
# bounded number of events however large the graph is.
#
# That points at the fix and it is not a GPU fix: raise the lookahead
# (it is physical - the rate limit - so this is a modelling question,
# not a tuning knob), or restructure so eligibility is not a global time
# band. Removing the two per-round device syncs (device-side loop or
# persistent kernel) is worth ~10x on the latency but cannot create
# width that is not there.
